===Etymology 1===
Originated in {{w|Jamaican Vernacular English}} or {{w|African American Vernacular English}}, probably originally a {{clipping|disrespect|lang=en|nocap=1}} or {{m|en|disparage}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|es}}

# {{lb|en|US|British|slang}} To put (someone) down, or show [[disrespect]] by the use of [[insulting]] [[language]] or [[dismissive]] [[behaviour]].
#* '''1905''', 10 December, ''The Sunday Times'' (Perth), "A New Word", [http://trove.nla.gov.au/ndp/del/article/57208246 page 4]:
#*: When a journalistic rival tries to "'''dis'''" you<br />And to prejudice you in the public's eyes.<br />Don't stigmatise his charges as a "tissue<br />Of palpable, unmitigated lies."

